I traced this through current `Sensor::Update()` and I think the issue is slightly broader than the large initial timestamp.

The current catch-up path is proportional to the number of missed sensor periods, so a UNIX-scale initial simulation time can require billions of increments.

There is also a second edge in the same code: the period is cast to whole milliseconds. For update rates above 1000 Hz, that can truncate to zero, which means the catch-up loop can never make forward progress.

I think the narrow root fix is:

1. Keep the period in `std::chrono::steady_clock::duration` instead of milliseconds.
2. Advance over missed periods arithmetically in O(1), rather than looping.
3. Guarantee the resulting period is at least one clock tick.

Conceptually:

```
next += delta;

if (next <= now)
{
  const auto missed = (now - next) / delta + 1;
  next += delta * missed;
}

```
This preserves the existing phase invariant: the next update remains the first scheduled tick strictly after `now`, rather than resetting scheduling relative to the current time.

I have a candidate fix here:

https://github.com/kvnloo/gz-sensors/tree/fix/sensor-update-catchup-662

I also added focused coverage for:

- a UNIX-scale absolute simulation time;
- a 2000 Hz sub-millisecond update period.

